{
  "is_sufficient": false,
  "knowledge_gaps": [
    "Training-prefix targets were already available during fitting. Their perturbation results measure in-sample sensitivity, not generalization or current user intent.",
    "The protocol leaves prefix selection, truncation, deletion counts, temporal windows, shuffle size, candidate sampling, repeated-target handling, and rank ties unspecified. Perturbations must operate on the same visible truncated history to avoid introducing previously unseen older items.",
    "Final-position extraction alone does not guarantee padding invariance: GRU biases can change hidden states while processing zero embeddings. The implementation must verify padding-state handling and preserve the documented B × L scoring interface.",
    "The adapter receives one category model at a time, but cross-category aggregation and bootstrap ownership are unspecified. Sample limits, runtime limits, and resource accounting are also missing.",
    "Originality 4/10 is reasonable, but potential 8/10 is speculative without measurements. Difficulty 3/10 understates interface verification and reproducibility work. A last-item-only input ablation of the same trained model would provide a useful additional control without introducing another predictor."
  ],
  "follow_up_queries": [
    {
      "reason": "Clarify recurrent padding behavior and scoring compatibility before interpreting temporal effects.",
      "query": "For PyTorch GRU4Rec with left-padded histories and per-position B × L × H features, how should padding steps be skipped or masked so added leading zeros cannot alter valid hidden states? Compare packed sequences with explicit masked recurrent updates, and describe invariance checks and B × L × K candidate scoring that preserve a per-position ModelView interface."
    },
    {
      "reason": "Make the perturbation experiment reproducible and distinguish memory utility from truncation artifacts, memorization, and synthetic distribution shifts.",
      "query": "What controls are recommended for deletion and order-perturbation diagnostics in sequential recommendation when next-item targets come from histories already used for training? Address perturbing only the original truncated window, retaining the final item, matched deletion counts, last-item-only input ablation of the same model, fixed unique candidates, repeated targets, deterministic rank ties, and limits on generalization claims."
    },
    {
      "reason": "Specify statistically interpretable aggregation within the shared compute budget and calibrate the proposal's expected value.",
      "query": "How should paired sequential-recommendation diagnostic effects be aggregated across four separately trained category models using one training prefix per sampled user? Describe stratified user bootstrap intervals for an equal-weight category macro mean, minimum slice counts, missing categories, bounded GPU diagnostic sampling, and limitations of confidence intervals after repeated validation-guided model search."
    }
  ]
}
